# CAMP-134: the questions that only have answers on the full dataset,
# asked on the machine that holds it.
#
# 🔴 WHY A TIMER AND NOT A GITHUB ACTIONS JOB. Until 04.11.2026 this was
# `.github/workflows/scale-check.yml` with `runs-on: [self-hosted,
# camptribe-data]`. The repository is PUBLIC, and a self-hosted runner for
# it would have to stand on the machine with the production database of
# personal data; GitHub's own documentation says self-hosted runners
# should almost never be used with public repositories, because any pull
# request can run code on them. No such runner was ever registered, so
# every nightly queued, waited and was cancelled — measured over 100 runs
# on 03.11.2026: 0 successful. The mechanism had not run ONCE.
#
# The same check, as a timer here, asks the same API over the same
# database and runs no code that did not come through a merged release.
#
# 🔴 WHAT RUNS: `--nightly --api-only`.
#   --nightly    rehearse first (prove the probe can still fail), then
#                probe — what the old workflow did in two steps.
#   --api-only   only what the API on :3001 can answer: the map index and
#                its chunks, and the rehearsal that drives the deleted
#                whole-world snapshot against it. NOT the OSM tag census:
#                it needs `osmium` and the merged extracts, and this
#                machine has neither (docs/deploy.md §6, measured
#                15.10.2026). Running it here would mean a letter to the
#                owner every night about a tool that is not installed —
#                a safeguard costing more than the defect it guards. The
#                census and its rehearsal are run by hand from the laptop
#                that holds the extracts; their LOGIC is checked on every
#                PR by `--self-test --offline` in scale-check.yml.
#
# 🔴 The two probes are seconds (2.2 s wall measured 27.09.2026), and the
# scale gate in run.sh refuses to proceed under 10 000 campsites, so a
# unit pointed at the wrong API fails rather than passes over a fixture.
# The journal line that carries the measured number is
# `scale-check against <N> campsites`, printed before any probe runs.
#
# 🔴 EnvironmentFile gives `API_BUILD_TOKEN` — the rate-limit bypass the
# bulk routes require — the same way the web build gets it: from the
# file, never from argv, because /proc on this machine is mounted without
# hidepid (docs/deploy.md). API_BASE_URL defaults to localhost:3001 in
# run.sh and is stated here so the unit says where it looks.
[Unit]
Description=CampTribe scale check against the full dataset
After=postgresql.service camptribe-api.service
Wants=camptribe-api.service
OnFailure=camptribe-failed@%n.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
User=camptribe
Group=camptribe
WorkingDirectory=/srv/camptribe/current
EnvironmentFile=/srv/camptribe/current/apps/api/.env
Environment=API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3001
# The probe asks the API, the API asks Postgres; nothing here competes
# with the site for long, but it must not win against it either.
Nice=10
TimeoutStartSec=20min
ExecStart=/bin/bash scripts/scale-check/run.sh --nightly --api-only
# 🔴 The trace is removed only after a SUCCESSFUL run. For Type=oneshot
# ExecStartPost does not run when ExecStart returned non-zero, so
# yesterday's alarm does not vanish because the unit merely started.
ExecStartPost=/bin/rm -f /srv/camptribe/failed/camptribe-scale-check.service
